    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nTo simplify an expression containing parentheses, we expand the parentheses and then collect like terms.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3(x + 2) + 4x]\n\nContent:\nOur first step is to distribute the [MATH: 3] over each of the terms in the parentheses:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n3(x + 2) + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n3\\cdot x + 3\\cdot 2 + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n(3x) + (6) + 4x &= \\[5pt]\n3x +6 + 4x &\n}]\n\nContent:\nThe second and final step is to collect the like terms:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n3x + 6 + 4x &= \\\n(3x + 4x) + 6 &= \\\n7x + 6\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Linear Expressions With Positive Numbers in Parentheses\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (4+c)\\cdot 3-8.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we distribute the [MATH: 3] over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n(4+c)\\cdot 3-8 &= \\[5pt]\n4\\cdot3+c\\cdot 3-8 &= \\[5pt]\n(12)+(3c)-8 &= \\[5pt]\n12+3c-8 &\n}]\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n[MATH: \\eqalign{\n12+3c-8 &= \\[5pt]\n3c +(12-8)&= \\[5pt]\n3c+4\n}]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2212 4x + 3(7x + y) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 17x + 3y]\nb. [MATH: 17x + y]\nc. [MATH: 21x + 3y]\nd. [MATH: 3x + y]\ne. [MATH: 3y \u2212 17x]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3u + (2u + v) \u22c5 4 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2u + v]\nb. [MATH: 11u + v]\nc. [MATH: 11u + 4v]\nd. [MATH: 3u \u2212 4v]\ne. [MATH: 8u + 4v]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Linear Expressions With Positive Numbers in Parentheses: Distributing a Negative Number\n\nExample Prompt:\n[MATH: (-4)(8x+7y) +30y=]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we distribute the [MATH: (-4)] over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n[MATH: (\u2212 4)(8x + 7y) + 30y|=; (\u2212 4) \u22c5 8x + (\u2212 4) \u22c5 7y + 30y|=; (\u2212 32x) + (\u2212 28y) + 30y|=; \u2212 32x \u2212 28y + 30y]\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n[MATH: \u2212 32x \u2212 28y + 30y|=; \u2212 32x + (\u2212 28y + 30y)|=; \u2212 32x + 2y]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)(6x + y) \u2212 4x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 22x \u2212 3y]\nb. [MATH: 22x + 3y]\nc. [MATH: 14x \u2212 3y]\nd. [MATH: 22x + 6y]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 22x + 6y]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 2u + (5u + v) \u22c5 (\u2212 4) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 16u \u2212 4v]\nb. [MATH: 18u \u2212 4v]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 16u \u2212 4v]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 16u + 4v]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 18u \u2212 4v]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Simplifying Linear Expressions With Negative Numbers in Parentheses\n\nExample Prompt:\nSimplify [MATH: (5 - y) \\cdot 3 - 10]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we distribute the [MATH: 3] over each of the terms in parentheses.\n\n[MATH: (5 \u2212 y) \u22c5 3 \u2212 10|=; 5 \u22c5 3 + (\u2212 y) \u22c5 3 \u2212 10|=; (15) + (\u2212 3y) \u2212 10|=; 15 \u2212 3y \u2212 10]\n\nThen, we collect like terms.\n\n[MATH: 15 \u2212 3y \u2212 10|=; (15 \u2212 10) \u2212 3y|=; 5 \u2212 3y]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3(x \u2212 5) \u2212 x =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 4x \u2212 5]\nb. [MATH: 2x \u2212 5]\nc. [MATH: 2x + 5]\nd. [MATH: 2x \u2212 15]\ne. [MATH: 2x + 15]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 6g + (f \u2212 2g) \u22c5 2 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: f \u2212 2g]\nb. [MATH: f + 2g]\nc. [MATH: 2f + 10g]\nd. [MATH: 2f + 2g]\ne. [MATH: 4fg]"
